course: dictionary
lesson: 9971
title: One word, two meanings: teacup
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A teacup is a cup for drinking tea, usually with a handle and a saucer under it. Read the words around it to find which meaning fits.
explain: «The Hatter is so nervous he bites a piece out of his teacup.» This is a silly, made-up moment, but the teacup is still the thing you drink from. In old recipes, teacup can mean an amount instead, so always check the sentence.
explain: Teacups also appear in phrases. «A storm in a teacup» means a lot of fuss about something small. Picture Mum and Auntie Sue wrapping Nan's teacups in newspaper: the word means the cups themselves, and the sentence tells you why they are being packed.
fact: A teacup is a cup for drinking tea, often with a handle and a saucer.
fact: Teacups appear at tea parties and at Gran's house.
fact: A teacup can also mean the amount a teacup holds, used in old recipes.
fact: «A storm in a teacup» means a lot of fuss about something small. In the US, people say «a tempest in a teapot».
fact: A teacup and saucer is a pair: the cup sits on the saucer.
exercise: «She poured hot tea into her teacup and added milk.» What is a teacup? | A small spoon for stirring sugar | *A cup used for drinking tea | A kind of bread | A large pot for boiling water
exercise: «Nan's teacups were wrapped in newspaper before the move.» What were the teacups wrapped in? | Birthday cards | Old curtains | Tea towels | *Newspaper
exercise: Jayden says the argument about the missing sock was a storm in a teacup. What does that mean? | *A lot of fuss about something small | A fight about who makes the tea | A sad story about a lost pet | A real storm with thunder and rain
test: Which phrase means a lot of fuss about something small? | Tea party | Bread-and-butter | *A storm in a teacup | Teacup and saucer
test: In an old recipe it says «add one teacup of sugar». What does teacup mean here? | *An amount, the amount a teacup holds | A saucer | A cup for drinking tea | A tea party
exam: Which sentence uses teacup to mean a cup for drinking tea? | *The Hatter held his teacup and bread-and-butter | Add half a teacup of milk | The recipe needs one teacup of flour | A teacup is a measure for old recipes
